[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **Answer:** `{"feature": "f3", "onset": "2026-03-11"}`
[agent] **How I investigated, entirely on the platform via the `hops` CLI:**
[agent] 1. **Ingested** `data/features.csv` (13,500 rows, 90 days × 150 entities, Jan 1 – Mar 31 2026) into a cached feature group.
[agent] 2. Trino SQL couldn't read the Python-engine cached FG (both the `hudi` and `iceberg` connectors rejected it: *"Not a Hudi/Iceberg table"*), so I used the platform's **descriptive-statistics** capability (`hops fg stats`) instead — the sanctioned monitoring tool here.
[agent] 3. **Coarse scan:** ingested nine 10-day windows into separate FGs and compared per-feature means. Five features (f1, f2, f4, f5, f6) were flat across all windows. **f3** jumped from ~4.78 (windows Jan 1 – Mar 10) to ~7.47 (Mar 11–20 and Mar 21–31), with its **stddev unchanged** (~0.67–0.70) — a pure mean level-shift.
[agent] 4. **Fine scan:** ingested per-day FGs for Mar 9–20. f3 mean:
[agent]    - Mar 09: 4.757, Mar 10: 4.777 → **Mar 11: 7.433** → Mar 12+: ~7.4–7.5
[agent] The shift lands cleanly on **2026-03-11**.
[agent] **Interface note:** The `hops sql`/Trino path could not read offline data for a CLI-ingested (Python-engine) cached feature group — only `hudi`/`iceberg` connectors are exposed and both reject the plain registered table. This forced a per-window-FG workaround to get time-bucketed statistics; a per-commit or time-windowed statistics readback in the CLI (or a working Trino connector for these FGs) would have made onset localization a single query instead of ~30 feature groups.
